<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Papak</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">died c. 210</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Pabag, Babak</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Papak, or Pabag, was a local ruler in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persis">Persis</a> in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> in the early 3rd century CE and father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardashir-i">Ardashir I</a>, the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>. According to tradition he was a priest at the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anahita">Anahita</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istakhr">Istakhr</a> who seized power in the city around 205, overthrowing the local king. The Sasanian kings honoured him as the founder of their house.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/papak.jpg" alt="Papak" width="320" height="312" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group; [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Pabag.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
